{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfenpmqKfY2gJs9poCr2E","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote the three files for candidate 2, and the self-check passed (`OK`). Its score on the short test fit is well behind the current best model: -1152.8 against -1047.1.\n\n**Model name:** `leaky_switch_impression_tracking`\n\n**Hypothesis:** People read a sequence flip by flip and keep a running sense of how often the coin is switching. That sense is held in a memory that fades, so recent flips count more than earlier ones. At every point in the reading they compare it with their own ideal switching rate. A sequence looks random to the extent that this running sense stays close to their ideal the whole way through. So a long streak pulls the impression well below the ideal for several flips. That makes a sequence whose switches are bunched together look less random than one with the same number of switches spread evenly.\n\nThe brief asked for a process-level account (here, a memory that fades). The aim was to close the critique's gap that people prefer the sequence with the shorter longest run more than the best model predicts. It doesn't try to address the other two gaps: the preference for heads-heavy sequences and the avoidance of almost-repeating patterns.\n\n**Model:**\n- **Ideal switching rate:** each person has their own, drawn from a population distribution with 400 participant slots.\n- **Forgetting rate:** one value shared by everyone, with a Beta(2,2) prior.\n- **Sensitivity:** one value shared by everyone.\n- **Computation:** the running impression is built with a weighting matrix over up to 7 flip-to-flip transitions. Positions beyond a shorter sequence's end are masked out.\n- **Choice:** the probability of choosing the left sequence comes from the difference between the two sequences' average squared distances from the ideal.\n\n**Self-check:** it loads correctly, follows the data contract, and gives a finite ELPD-LOO. The check doesn't cover three things admission will test:\n- **Convergence:** not verified, because the test fit ran only one chain, so R-hat couldn't be computed.\n- **Reliability of the score:** 5.1% of trials had Pareto k above 0.5, over the 1% tolerance, so the -1152.8 may be inaccurate.\n- **Novelty and speed:** not checked against the other models. Speed may be a concern, since even the short 100-draw fit took 146 seconds and the full fit must finish within 30 minutes.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run2/data/subjective_randomness/experiment1/model_loop/iter_4/candidate_2/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3146,"cache_read_input_tokens":62920,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3146,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3ff090fa-e749-46b8-bdd9-540c326a9e5d","uuid":"bd8627ec-a673-4f47-abc4-af000544afd1","timestamp":"2026-10-03T05:58:29.600Z","request_id":"req_011CfenpkuGhJjkK1NxVNMgQ"}
